## Phoenix rejects a tracing configuration that could silently drop spans

Source: Arize Phoenix — https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/releases/tag/%40arizeai/phoenix-otel%403.1.0
Published: 2026-11-17
Channel: github

Arize Phoenix is an open-source platform for observing and evaluating AI applications. Its tracing package helps configure OpenTelemetry, which records operations as spans. The release notes describe a configuration mistake that could drop every span silently when an exporter was passed positionally to a span processor instead of inside an options object.

The updated batch and simple processors now throw a type error for that positional form, making the mistake visible. The registration helper also accepts a sampler, which controls which traces are recorded, and span limits, and re-exports several sampler implementations. It warns once per applicable environment variable when settings for sampling, spans, attributes or batch processing are set but the corresponding option is not configured in code, because the underlying OpenTelemetry JavaScript release ignores those variables. For batch settings with custom processors, the warning comes from the re-exported batch processor. The release notes do not report how many deployments encountered the issue.

Why it matters: JavaScript teams upgrading Phoenix tracing should check processor construction and heed warnings about ignored telemetry settings.

